          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Hartogs number
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is the number of ways to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-order
            a set of cardinality at most
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            Assuming the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            whose cardinality is greater than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            and therefore the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/successor"} successor
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            as a cardinal number. But even without the axiom of choice, it makes sense and is often an effective substitute for such a successor.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We will define the Hartogs number as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorial
            operation from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordered+sets"} well-ordered sets
          %p
            So let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be a set. Without the axiom of choice (or more precisely, the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            itself, but we can certainly well-order some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (or a subset), then there may be many different ways to do so, even non
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            ways. So to begin with, let us form the collection of all well-ordered subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi S
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_{A: \mathcal{P}S} \mathcal{P}(A \times A) ,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod
            indicates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            indicates
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,R)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a well-ordering. Then form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            by identifying all well-ordered subsets that are isomorphic as well-ordered sets. This gives a set of well-order types, or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
          %p
            The
            %strong Hartogs number
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is this well-ordered set, the set of all order types of well-ordered subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is the cardinality of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi κ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa^+
            be the cardinality or ordinal rank (as desired) of the Hartogs number of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong Hartogs number
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            There are other ways to encode
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq S
            with a well-ordering can be specified through the collection of its initial segments or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+set"} down-sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            named by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P P(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            can be defined as a suitable subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P P(S)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P P(S)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P P P(S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
            which is the set of such equivalence classes is definable as a subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P P P(S)
          %p
            All of the defining formulas in this description involve only bounded quantification, i.e., can be expressed in the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} BZC
            or in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
            is well-ordered (by existence of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/well-founded+relation#coalgebraic_formulation"} coalgebra
            morphisms between representatives of order types) can be enacted in these theories without using the axiom of choice, hence the basic theory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
            can be carried out in BZ (bounded Zermelo set theory, without choice).
          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              There is no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S) \to S
          %p
            This theorem is to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%27s+theorem"} Cantor's theorem
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burali-Forti%27s+paradox"} Burali-Forti's paradox
            is to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell%27s+paradox"} Russell's paradox
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              As observed before,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
              is well-ordered. Suppose there were an injection
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℵ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: \aleph(S) \to S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              gives a bijection of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph(S)
              onto its image
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              uniquely admits a well-ordering for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is an isomorphism of well-ordered sets. Using this well-ordering, there is an initial segment inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: I \hookrightarrow \aleph(S)
            %p
              which takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in I
              to (the order type of)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                      %mo :
                      %mi y
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
